The idea behind is_prime
To test whether n is prime, try dividing it by every number from 2 up to n - 1. If any of them divides evenly (remainder 0), then n is not prime.
def is_prime(n):
    if n < 2:                    # 0, 1 and negatives are not prime
        return False
    for i in range(2, n):
        if n % i == 0:           # found a number that divides evenly
            return False         # so n is NOT prime, stop right here
    return True                  # nobody divided it evenly, so it IS prime

Why does return False inside the loop work so well? The moment return runs, the function ends immediately. You don't need a break or a flag. If the loop finishes without ever finding a divisor, the last line return True runs.
Trace is_prime(9):

i     9 % i      Result
2      1         not a divisor, continue
3      0         divides evenly → return False

Trace is_prime(7): it tries 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, none divide evenly, so the loop ends and return True runs.
The idea behind primes_in_range
def primes_in_range(start, end):
    primes = []
    for num in range(start, end + 1):    # end + 1 so that end is included
        if is_prime(num):                # calling your own function
            primes.append(num)
    return primes

Notice how one function calls another. This is the real power of functions: is_prime is written once and reused.

